# browser-test output (the committed board image is docs/screenshot.png)
ui-test.png

# --- Credential files (#12) ------------------------------------------------
# `git add .` is the normal workflow, so this file is the only thing between
# that and a committed private key. The CI guard catches a tracked .key/.pem
# AFTER the fact; ignoring them up front is cheaper, and these four names are
# not caught by that guard at all — it matches extensions, and .netrc/pgpass
# have none.
#
# Every pattern below is asserted by `npm run check:ignore`, which asks git
# itself rather than grepping this file. Delete a line and that check names the
# file that became committable.
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks

# SSH private keys, including the ones an operator drops in from a host.
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519

# Curl/machine credentials and postgres's password file. Both are bare
# name==password, stored in the home directory, and both are routinely
# symlinked or copied into a project tree by tooling that does not ask.
.netrc
_netrc
pgpass
.pgpass

# Generic secret blobs that tooling drops next to the code.
secrets.json
credentials.json

# Every other .env variant (.env.local, .env.production, ...) — but NEVER
# .env.example itself. It is the file an operator is told to copy, so it has to
# stay tracked; without this negation the glob above would silently exclude the
# one .env file in the repo that is supposed to be public. check:ignore asserts
# that .env.example is still NOT ignored.
.env.*
!.env.example

# Companion odds recorder (a separate Python project, ~79MB). It carries its
# own .gitignore, but it lives INSIDE this working tree with no git repo of its
# own, so `git add .` from here would stage it — including
# betfair-market-snapshots/certs/*.key, which are real Betfair bot login keys.
# Never commit it. Clone it separately; docs/FEED.md describes the contract.
betfair-market-snapshots/
